+# Decision: Concurrency Model for Competing Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+A visit is edited offline on a shared tablet and can go days between being fetched and being submitted. Two workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes, so the server regularly receives two submissions that both started from the same prior state. The server has no botanical knowledge: it cannot tell whether two edited fields are unrelated (an observer-name fix and a missing species) or scientifically linked (a species identification and its cover estimate). This decision sets how such competing submissions become one current record.
+
+Every option here is required to retain every original submission; none may resolve a conflict by discarding one worker's input. That requirement is inherited from the project and does not distinguish between the options below.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Scientific-relationship risk** (major) — tenet: *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable.* A record that combines two submissions' fields without a human looking at the pair is no longer inspectable if the combination happens to cross a relationship the server didn't know about.
+- **Review workload** (major) — the team runs on two coordinators handling roughly 6,000 visits a season; a policy that routes too much to manual review risks a queue the team can't clear before the weekly export.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden** (moderate) — tenet: *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator.* A policy whose correctness depends on a maintained rule set (per field type, kept current as the schema evolves) pulls toward needing that administrator.
+- **Export readiness by Friday** (moderate) — goal: *Make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export.* A policy that clears more visits to a settled state without review gets more of the season's data in front of the partner sooner.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each submission after a visit's first names the base revision it started from. A submission landing on the visit's current revision becomes the new current revision directly. A submission landing on a stale base becomes a competing revision: it and every sibling submission sharing that stale base go into the visit's unresolved review work, untouched, while the prior current revision keeps serving as the last-known state. A coordinator resolves unresolved work by designating one competing revision as current; nothing is combined on the server's own initiative. A worker or coordinator wanting the best of two competing revisions submits a further hand-edit based on the designated one, through the ordinary submission path.
+
+- Scientific-relationship risk: **strong fit** — no field is ever combined without a coordinator's eyes on the full pair of submissions, so the server never guesses at a relationship it can't see.
+- Review workload: **weak fit** — any two submissions that touch the same visit concurrently become review work, including edits to fields that happen to be unrelated.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **strong fit** — the server compares revision IDs; there is no per-field rule set to keep correct as the schema changes.
+- Export readiness by Friday: **partial fit** — unresolved visits are withheld from export until resolved, which is visible but can concentrate work right before the deadline if submissions cluster late in the week.
+
+Fewer automated outcomes; a coordinator sees every concurrent edit, related or not.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each submission supplies the base state it started from and the changes it made. When two submissions against the same base touch disjoint fields, the server combines them into one new revision without review. When they touch the same field, or when a maintained rule for that field type (scalar fields, the observer list, species entries) can't establish disjointness, the submissions go to review instead. Every combined record still carries both contributing submissions in history.
+
+- Scientific-relationship risk: **weak fit** — disjoint-field combination is exactly the case where two submissions can be scientifically linked despite touching different fields, and the server emits a combined record without a coordinator having seen the pair. Requiring manual approval of the combined record doesn't close this gap; the approval reviews a record that already looks plausible, not the two originals side by side.
+- Review workload: **strong fit** — only genuinely overlapping edits reach the queue, which for 24 field workers correcting distinct scientific fields is likely to be the minority of concurrent edits.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **weak fit** — scalar fields, the observer list, and species entries each need their own combination rule, and a new scientific field type added later needs a new rule before it can be trusted to merge safely.
+- Export readiness by Friday: **strong fit** — most concurrent edits clear automatically, so more of the week's visits are export-ready without coordinator attention.
+
+Smaller review queue and faster export readiness, paid for with rules the team must keep correct as the record's fields evolve.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.** Scientific-relationship risk is the deciding criterion: it is major, and it is the one place the two options genuinely diverge in kind rather than degree — field-aware merging's weak fit there isn't a tuning problem, it's the option's premise, because the policy's entire value proposition is deciding when combination is safe using only field names. The *inspectable path* tenet weighs a record that might encode a relationship no one checked more heavily than a larger review queue; the team confirmed it will accept that trade when the two were framed directly against each other. Review workload and export readiness are real and both cut toward field-aware merge, but neither rises to major, and the *coordinators without a dedicated administrator* tenet favors the option that carries no rule set to maintain in the first place.
+
+This forecloses automatic combination of any kind in this node's first version: tablet-sync must retain the base revision ID a draft was started from and be able to show a worker that their submission became a competing revision rather than the current one; review-export must build a revision-comparison view for unresolved visits and exclude any visit with unresolved work from the CSV until a coordinator resolves it. Neither node gets to assume a visit is single-revision by the time it reaches them.
+
+Field-aware merging is worth re-litigating if the review queue this model produces turns out to exceed what two coordinators can clear before a Friday export on a normal week, or if a season of resolved-work data shows that most competing revisions in practice touch disjoint fields — at that point the risk this decision weighted as major would be trading against a review burden that has become the more pressing problem.
