-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Concurrency Model: Whole-Visit Revisions
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+A visit's history is a chain of revisions. Each submission after the first names the **base revision ID** — the revision its author started from. The first submission for a visit has no base.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+A submission whose base names the visit's current revision becomes the new current revision directly: no other submission has moved the visit since the author started, so there is nothing to reconcile. A submission whose base does not name the current revision is a **competing revision** — some other submission became current after this author started. Competing revisions are stored, never discarded, and placed into the visit's unresolved review work alongside every other revision sharing that same stale base. The prior current revision remains the last-known current state while the visit is unresolved.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+This node does not attempt to combine competing revisions. Two edits to unrelated-looking fields (an observer correction, a missing species) may in fact record a related scientific judgment the field names do not expose; whole-visit revisions avoid guessing at that relationship by routing every concurrent edit to a coordinator rather than combining any of it automatically.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+A coordinator resolves unresolved review work by designating one of the competing revisions as the visit's current revision. The other competing revision(s) remain in submission history, retained but no longer current. If neither competing revision is adequate on its own, a worker or coordinator submits a further correcting edit based on the designated revision, through the ordinary submission path — this node provides no separate merge operation.
+
+## Submission and Response Shapes
+
+**Submission envelope**: visit ID, plot reference, base revision ID (absent for a visit's first submission), idempotency key, author ID, and the visit's scientific fields (observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, notes).
+
+**Current-state response**: for a visit with no unresolved review work, the current revision and its ID. For a visit with unresolved review work, an unresolved indicator together with the competing revisions (their IDs, authors, and receipt times) and the last-known current revision.
+
+**Resolution operation**: a coordinator ID, the visit ID, and the ID of the competing revision being designated current. Recorded with the contributing submission IDs per REC-006.
 
 ## Downstream Contract Work
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+Tablet-sync sizes its local storage and retry state machine around the submission envelope: it retains the base revision ID a draft was started from and, on a competing-revision outcome, has a pending revision to show the worker. Review-export sizes its coordinator interface around the current-state response's unresolved indicator: it needs a revision comparison view and must exclude visits with unresolved review work from the export set. Both are designed in later sessions and must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency policy for competing visit edits | Whole-visit revisions: a submission is accepted onto its stated base; two submissions sharing a stale base become competing revisions a coordinator resolves by selection, not merge | Field-aware three-way merge: disjoint field edits combine automatically, overlapping edits go to review | See [decisions/concurrency-model.md](decisions/concurrency-model.md). |
